$DIR/main.p:13:20: v.Slice() holds a view of v (v.Slice(), line 13), which may have been passed to v.Push(j), which may free what it looks at or move it out (rt.MoveValues(&v.data[0], &old[0], len(old)), vec.p:434) at line 15, so v.Slice() cannot be used here
    v.Slice() looks at memory that v owns, without owning it: the view was made
    by v.Slice() at line 13. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be
    used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. A call
    that may free memory the view looks at, or move an owner out of it, through
    a pointer to the variable or to memory it owns ends the view as well. At
    line 15 v was passed to v.Push(j), which may free what it looks at or move
    it out (rt.MoveValues(&v.data[0], &old[0], len(old)), vec.p:434) on some
    paths to this point, so the view may look at memory that was released, or at
    a value that now belongs to something else. The range statement evaluates
    its operand once and reads the next element through it at each iteration,
    after the body has run.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use v.Slice() before line 15, or make that call only after the last use of
    v.Slice()
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
